{
  "identifier": "NG6622",
  "full_title": "Saint Gregory the Great with Saints Ignatius Loyola and Francis Xavier",
  "short_title": "Saint Gregory the Great with Jesuit Saints",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "baroque",
  "material": "oil on canvas",
  "dimensions": "296 × 211 cm",
  "production_date": "about 1625-6",
  "brief_description": "This altarpiece commemorates one of the most important events of the papacy of Pope Gregory XV (born Alessandro Ludovisi). It shows the Jesuits Ignatius Loyola and Francis Xavier being canonised (officially declared saints in the Roman Catholic Church). This occurred on 12 March 1622, the feast day of Saint Gregory the Great.\n\nRegarded as one of the Doctors, or Fathers, of the Western Church, Gregory the Great was pope from 590 to 604. Here he is seated on an elevated throne between Saint Ignatius Loyola, Spanish founder of the Society of Jesus (or Jesuits), on the left and Saint Francis Xavier, the first great Jesuit missionary, on the right.\n\nThe painting was likely commissioned by the Ludovisi family after Gregory XV’s death in 1623 to honour his close connections with the Jesuit order. The Ludovisi were one of Bologna’s most distinguished families and early patrons of Guercino.",
  "accession_date": "2013-01-07",
  "current_location": "Room 32, The National Gallery (London)",
